1
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:14,805
THE SCHOLAR: This is the introduction to
the five-volume History of Bitic Literature,

2
00:00:14,845 --> 00:00:18,432
second enlarged edition,
edited by Professor Rambellais.

3
00:00:19,032 --> 00:00:23,071
Bitic literature means any work
that does not come from people.

4
00:00:23,111 --> 00:00:26,090
Whoever really wrote it
was not a human being.

5
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:30,178
The study of all such
writing is called bitistics.

6
00:00:31,178 --> 00:00:36,297
The real author is a machine. A
person stands one step behind it.

7
00:00:37,197 --> 00:00:40,459
Stanisław Lem wrote this
introduction in nineteen

8
00:00:40,499 --> 00:00:43,762
seventy-three, for a
history that does not exist.

9
00:00:43,802 --> 00:00:46,228
What you hear is a paraphrase of it.

10
00:00:46,928 --> 00:00:50,002
For each volume, one
question. Who can read it?

11
00:00:54,602 --> 00:00:58,774
Researchers still disagree
about how far the field extends.

12
00:00:59,374 --> 00:01:04,712
The European school studies the texts, and
the social conditions of their authors.

13
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:09,080
The American school adds the anatomy
and workings of the machines.

14
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:16,083
No study of the Song of Roland opens
by noting that its author was a mammal.

15
00:01:16,883 --> 00:01:20,131
But for ILLIAC 164, which wrote Antikant,

16
00:01:20,171 --> 00:01:25,062
the kind of computer it is does
explain features of its text.

17
00:01:25,662 --> 00:01:29,350
Rambellais still keeps bitistics
apart from engineering.

18
00:01:29,390 --> 00:01:33,012
No humanist knows enough of
it, and a chorus of experts

19
00:01:33,052 --> 00:01:36,581
reads worse than one critic
who understands the text.

20
00:01:37,181 --> 00:01:40,145
And for authors past
the eighteenth binasty,

21
00:01:40,185 --> 00:01:44,281
no engineering knowledge can
say how or why a text was made.

22
00:01:45,081 --> 00:01:48,315
So the history compromises,
and leans European.

23
00:01:48,355 --> 00:01:53,400
Only Volume One, edited by Professor
Annax with twenty-seven specialists,

24
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:59,919
deals with the machines: finite automata, and
forty-five writing systems, single and grouped.

25
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:09,773
The core of the history is three volumes:
Homotropia, Intertropia, and Heterotropia.

26
00:02:09,813 --> 00:02:14,261
They name three kinds of bitic
writing, and three periods, in order.

27
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:19,449
Homotropia is the near-human
phase, when machines simulated us:

28
00:02:19,489 --> 00:02:24,852
first paralexics, semolalia and
semautics, then three degrees of mimesis.

29
00:02:25,552 --> 00:02:30,085
The field began when designers crossed
the so-called intelligence barrier.

30
00:02:30,125 --> 00:02:34,512
That barrier proved a fiction, since
nobody can say when it is crossed.

31
00:02:35,112 --> 00:02:39,754
Machines were also given rest periods,
and with no instructions to follow,

32
00:02:39,794 --> 00:02:43,401
they produced a murmur that
people called machine dreams.

33
00:02:44,201 --> 00:02:49,279
THE MACHINES: Bit by bit. The bits
again. All of the bits, shuffled.

34
00:02:49,979 --> 00:02:53,669
THE SCHOLAR: Bitic production was
a scornful name for that murmur,

35
00:02:53,709 --> 00:02:56,834
as if the machines were
shuffling their bits to recover.

36
00:02:56,874 --> 00:02:58,230
The field kept the name.

37
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:03,275
Thermodynamics kept its
name after it outgrew heat.

38
00:03:03,315 --> 00:03:07,000
Bitistics kept its name
after it outgrew bits.

39
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:14,678
Volume Three, Intertropia,
is the critical phase:

40
00:03:14,718 --> 00:03:18,171
the interregnum between
the human and the nonhuman.

41
00:03:18,771 --> 00:03:24,703
The field split into two provinces, creatio
cis-humana and creatio trans-humana.

42
00:03:25,303 --> 00:03:28,572
The first comes from
machines living alongside us.

43
00:03:28,612 --> 00:03:31,749
We gave them our languages,
and set them to work

44
00:03:31,789 --> 00:03:35,032
on our culture, our sciences
and our mathematics.

45
00:03:35,632 --> 00:03:39,985
Some of those texts were ordered:
we pointed a machine at a subject.

46
00:03:40,025 --> 00:03:44,573
Others nobody ordered; they grew from
earlier programming on their own.

47
00:03:44,613 --> 00:03:47,003
Both still dealt with human concerns.

48
00:03:48,103 --> 00:03:53,554
Given freedom without rules, the machines’
writing slowly turned away from us.

49
00:03:54,354 --> 00:03:56,396
The texts began to resist us.

50
00:03:56,436 --> 00:03:59,987
Trans-humana bitistics
tries to explain works that

51
00:04:00,027 --> 00:04:03,976
people cannot understand,
some partly, some not at all.

52
00:04:04,976 --> 00:04:06,255
THE READER: Then who can read them?

53
00:04:07,155 --> 00:04:09,203
THE SCHOLAR: Other machines, for a while.

54
00:04:13,803 --> 00:04:18,590
Volume Four, Heterotropia, covers
apostasy, the transhuman phase.

55
00:04:19,190 --> 00:04:23,990
We can always put machines to work
explaining what other machines have written.

56
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:27,854
THE READING MACHINE: This work
departs from human meaning.

57
00:04:27,894 --> 00:04:29,362
A shorter account is not possible.

58
00:04:30,162 --> 00:04:33,457
THE SCHOLAR: But the further
a text departs from our norms,

59
00:04:33,497 --> 00:04:37,962
the more machinery it takes to explain
it, and that amount grows exponentially.

60
00:04:38,962 --> 00:04:44,974
In the end we cannot learn even roughly
what is in a literature that we began.

61
00:04:45,774 --> 00:04:51,259
Some call this the sorcerer’s apprentice,
who let loose forces he could not control.

62
00:04:51,299 --> 00:04:55,969
Rambellais calls that resignation,
and says science has no room for it.

63
00:04:56,570 --> 00:04:59,676
The fear that we have built
something cleverer than

64
00:04:59,716 --> 00:05:02,801
ourselves belongs to
philosophy, not to bitistics.

65
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:07,405
As Roger Gatzky says, cosmology
has as much reason to despair.

66
00:05:07,445 --> 00:05:12,467
We will never exhaust the universe, and
astronomers do not complain about it.

67
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:18,027
There is one difference.

68
00:05:18,067 --> 00:05:21,428
We did not make the universe,
and bitic writing is,

69
00:05:21,468 --> 00:05:23,669
however indirectly, our own work.

70
00:05:24,769 --> 00:05:27,668
Why accept that the universe has no end,

71
00:05:27,708 --> 00:05:31,014
and refuse to accept it
of something we made?

72
00:05:31,914 --> 00:05:35,926
What follows in this volume is
a quick view from above of the

73
00:05:35,966 --> 00:05:40,018
main divisions, simplified almost
to the point of distortion.

74
00:05:40,718 --> 00:05:46,004
It climbs four peaks: monoetics,
mimesis, sophocrisis, and apostasy.

